"I attempted the reproduction — and the results are important: **the bug as described does not reproduce on a well-formed CSV, but I did find a closely related case where the field does get split.**\n\n## Attempt 1: Standard quoted field with comma (the exact reported case) — no bug\n\n```\n=== raw file ===\nname,age,city\n\"Smith, John\",34,Springfield\n\n=== tool output ===\nTotal rows: 1\nTotal columns: 3\nFirst data row: ['Smith, John', '34', 'Springfield']\n```\n\nThe quoted comma is handled correctly: 3 columns, `Smith, John` intact. This is expected — the tool uses Python's `csv.reader`, which implements proper quote handling, and `test_quoted_field_with_comma` in the test suite covers exactly this.\n\n## Attempt 2: Same field with one space before the opening quote — field splits\n\nThis is the common real-world CSV style (e.g., hand-written or some exports put a space after each comma):\n\n```\n=== raw file (cat -A, $ = line end) ===\nname,age,city$\n \"Smith, John\",34,Springfield$\n\n=== tool output (incorrect) ===\nTotal rows: 1\nTotal columns: 3      <- header says 3, data row has 4 fields\nFirst data row: [' \"Smith', ' John\"', '34', 'Springfield']\n```\n\nHere the field **is** incorrectly split into `[' \"Smith', ' John\"']` — the quoted comma splits the field across two columns.\n\n## Why\n\nWith the default CSV dialect (`skipinitialspace=False`), a quote is only treated as a quote character when it is the **first character of the field**. A single space before `\"` (`␣\"Smith, John\"`) makes the quote a literal character, so the comma inside acts as a real delimiter. This matches RFC 4180 semantics — the file is technically malformed — but it's a very common real-world pattern and would look to a user exactly like \"quoted fields with commas get split.\"\n\n## Conclusion and options\n\n- The tool is **correct** for well-formed quoted fields; the report likely involves files with spaces before opening quotes (or possibly a different delimiter, e.g., `;`-separated exports — happy to check that too).\n- Possible fixes, with a trade-off to be aware of:\n  1. `skipinitialspace=True` — fixes the space-before-quote case, but strips leading spaces from *unquoted* fields, which conflicts with the original requirement to preserve exact field content.\n  2. Leave parsing as-is and document the limitation.\n\nIf you can share the actual CSV the user hit, I can confirm which case it is; otherwise tell me which fix you prefer and I'll implement and test it."